On Apr 20, 5:15 am, ManaUser <p...@manauserbanana.info> wrote:
> What if instead of turning into a statue,
> polymorphed boulder turned into a wall?

Sounds reasonable.

> The details:
> If it's in a doorway or room square ("."), it turns into a wall, if
> it's in a corridor ("#"), then solid rock. It wouldn't work if on top
> of a dungeon feature such as stairs, and altar or a fountain.

I would still give it a random chance of becoming a statue: Say, 30%
statue, 70% wall/rock/pillar. Just makes the game a little less
predictable, and it's also nice to have a source of random statues
sometimes. Of course with multiple boulders on one square, as often
happens when you kill giants, you could end up with a half-dozen
statues stuck in a wall/column of rock.
You kick the wall! --more--
A statue of a mastodon comes loose --more--
you are crushed under a falling statue! -- more--
DYWYPI!

> If it was touching a wall the message would with be:
> The shifting boulder fuses with the walls!

> If it was touching solid rock the message would with be:
> The shifting boulder fuses with the surrounding stone!

> If blocked by a dungeon feature:
> The boulder looks liquidly for a moment.

> If non of the above:
> The shifting boulder fuses with the floor!

and ceiling!


> If hallucinating:
> That boulder just went all non-Euclidean!

> If unseen:
> You hear a slushing sound.

> The only part I'm not sure about is what should happen if a monster
> was there. Either it it could fail, as with a dungeon feature, it
> could get stuck, or just for fun, the boulder could fuse with it and
> create a stone golem instead of a wall.

Or, it could statufy the monster. Seems like a cheap way to do away
with Titans and giants though, who will often stand on boulders to
pick them up. Then again, if you have a means of polymorphing them,
you have a means of rendering them (relatively) harmless anyway (since
there aren't many things a Titan can turn into that *aren't*
relatively harmless) so maybe it's not too much of an issue.

Again though, I'd prefer an "any of the above" approach - say, a 5%
chance of stoning the monster, a 5% chance of golemnifying it, a 30%
chance of the boulder turning to a random statue (monster unaffected)
and a 60% chance of being stuck in the wall. If there is an adjacent
non-wall square they should be able to struggle free after a few
turns. If they are completely buried in solid rock with no adjacent
space they should die, unless they have a means of digging, in which
case they should use it to free themselves.



